From 782973f56a6c905496eaa435c6240f78bf9a4c04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Skippy contributors <ci@skippy.local>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 02:26:37 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 51/52] llama: let recurrent memory hold states for sequences
 that do not run

A recurrent memory has one cell per sequence and finds a sequence's cell
at cells[seq_id], so every sequence id must be below the number of
cells. Skippy keeps resident prefixes under cache sequence ids past
n_seq_max, so their recurrent state cannot stay in the memory: every
save copies it out to the host and every restore copies it back.

Each sequence's cell is now recorded in a tails table of its own, and
n_rs_resident, a Skippy build input beside kv_rows, adds cells beyond
n_seq_max. A memory with those cells accepts every sequence id a unified
cache does.
seq_cp already lets sequences share a cell, and the first decode of a
sharing sequence moves it to a free cell through the s_copy gather the
graph runs anyway, so a sequence that never runs can hold a state
without it being copied.

With n_rs_resident = 0, the default, the memory has the same cells and
accepts the same sequence ids as before.
